1
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:02,959
[instrumental music]

2
00:00:04,091 --> 00:00:06,484
[bell ringing]

3
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:17,191
High school, huh? Must bring
back some memories for you.

4
00:00:17,278 --> 00:00:18,583
Friday nights,
your friends from band

5
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,542
strapping on
those funny hats.

6
00:00:20,629 --> 00:00:22,457
You know what?
I am not gonna take the bait.

7
00:00:22,544 --> 00:00:24,763
And then after practice,
a little spin the bottle..

8
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:27,070
in some wood paneled
rec room.

9
00:00:27,157 --> 00:00:28,767
Whatever you say.

10
00:00:32,032 --> 00:00:34,034
Rigsby,
what have we got?

11
00:00:34,121 --> 00:00:36,645
JackMcTierney, 28.
English teacher.

12
00:00:36,732 --> 00:00:38,168
ME, puts the time of death

13
00:00:38,255 --> 00:00:40,475
between 6 o'clock
and 9 o'clock last night.

14
00:00:40,562 --> 00:00:42,259
Apparently he likes to
stay late and go jogging

15
00:00:42,346 --> 00:00:43,913
around the campus.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,306
Cho's searching
his locker now.

17
00:00:45,393 --> 00:00:47,134
[sighs]

18
00:00:47,221 --> 00:00:48,700
Looks like it's a blunt-force
trauma to the head.

19
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:50,050
'We have a murder weapon?'

20
00:00:50,137 --> 00:00:51,790
No, but the ME thinks
it was metal.

21
00:00:51,877 --> 00:00:53,749
When we got here, there were
bats in the equipment bins.

22
00:00:53,836 --> 00:00:55,098
We're testing them
for blood now.

23
00:00:55,185 --> 00:00:56,404
Did we contact the family?

24
00:00:56,491 --> 00:00:57,448
Yeah, but they're all
back East.

25
00:00:57,535 --> 00:00:58,797
He's single,
lives alone.

26
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:00,582
Alright, start setting
up interviews

27
00:01:00,669 --> 00:01:02,540
'anybody who was here between
6:00 and 9:00 last night.'

28
00:01:02,627 --> 00:01:04,151
You got it.

29
00:01:04,238 --> 00:01:06,153
Look at that bruising
around the eye.

30
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,156
Mm. It's faded. Looks like
it's about a week old.

31
00:01:09,243 --> 00:01:11,723
Be good to know
how that happened.

32
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:13,899
Well, let's ask
the principal.

33
00:01:13,986 --> 00:01:15,553
That guy?

34
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:18,948
Cheap power suit, phone holster,
Bluetooth, large watch.

35
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:20,776
Has petty tyrant
written all over him.

36
00:01:20,863 --> 00:01:22,647
Excuse me! Sir?

37
00:01:22,734 --> 00:01:24,693
Hi. I'm Agent Lisbon.
This is Patrick Jane.

38
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,173
We're with the CBI.

39
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,131
Principal Carl Snyder.

40
00:01:28,218 --> 00:01:31,047
Thank you so much
for coming so quickly.

41
00:01:31,134 --> 00:01:33,354
Uh, did Mr. McTierney
have any problems?

42
00:01:33,441 --> 00:01:34,398
Trouble with a student?

43
00:01:34,485 --> 00:01:35,486
No, I don't think so.

44
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:36,879
He was a great teacher.

45
00:01:36,966 --> 00:01:39,142
He started working here
right after college.

46
00:01:39,229 --> 00:01:41,144
Uh, bright kid.
Could have made money

47
00:01:41,231 --> 00:01:43,233
doing so many things,
but he loved teaching.

48
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,148
We were lucky to have him.

49
00:01:45,235 --> 00:01:47,977
Now I have a
grief counseling session

50
00:01:48,064 --> 00:01:49,892
- starting momentarily--
- Excellent. We'll come.

51
00:01:49,979 --> 00:01:53,069
Uh, how did he get
the black eye?

52
00:01:53,156 --> 00:01:55,593
He said that he,
was playing basketball

53
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:56,812
with some students
after school.

54
00:01:56,899 --> 00:01:58,466
But you didn't believe him?

55
00:01:58,553 --> 00:02:00,032
Well, why would he lie?
Of course I believed him.

56
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:01,382
No, you didn't.

57
00:02:01,469 --> 00:02:03,123
Well, why would Ilie?

58
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:04,994
Probably just
bureaucratic instinct.

59
00:02:05,081 --> 00:02:06,735
Now if you suspected
that the black eye story

60
00:02:06,822 --> 00:02:09,520
was false, did you have
any notion of the true story?

61
00:02:09,607 --> 00:02:11,261
- No.
- Hmm?

62
00:02:11,348 --> 00:02:13,742
Like I said, he was very
popular among everyone

63
00:02:13,829 --> 00:02:15,352
students and faculty alike.

64
00:02:15,439 --> 00:02:17,224
Not everyone. I went
through McTierney's locker.

65
00:02:17,311 --> 00:02:19,704
Nothing unusual. But I found
this in his briefcase.

66
00:02:22,272 --> 00:02:24,274
[instrumental music]

67
00:02:26,276 --> 00:02:28,365
Oh. This is
very interesting.

68
00:02:30,324 --> 00:02:32,456
I had the honor of working
with Jack McTierney

69
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:34,197
for the past five years.

70
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:35,807
He was a good teacher.

71
00:02:36,982 --> 00:02:39,202
And he will be missed.

72
00:02:39,289 --> 00:02:41,813
Thank you, Mr. Loveland.

73
00:02:41,900 --> 00:02:46,383
Now is there anyone else who
would like to say something

74
00:02:46,470 --> 00:02:47,863
about Mr. McTierney?

75
00:02:53,260 --> 00:02:55,262
We-well, I'd-I'd
like to say something.

76
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:59,918
Mr. Jane, this is a very
sensitive moment.

77
00:03:00,005 --> 00:03:02,443
Yeah, they're just teenagers.
They'll get over it.

78
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:04,575
[Patrick clears throat]

79
00:03:04,662 --> 00:03:06,577
Good morning, everyone.
I'm Patrick Jane.

80
00:03:06,664 --> 00:03:08,666
I'm with the CBI.

81
00:03:08,753 --> 00:03:11,016
I'm sorry for
everyone's loss. Uh..

82
00:03:11,103 --> 00:03:12,670
I know you were close
to Mr. McTierney

83
00:03:12,757 --> 00:03:14,933
and I was hoping
that one of you could tell me

84
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:17,240
who wrote this letter
that we found.

85
00:03:17,327 --> 00:03:18,763
[rustling]

86
00:03:21,636 --> 00:03:22,593
"Dear Mr. McT..

87
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,291
[sighs]

88
00:03:24,378 --> 00:03:26,423
'"I saw what you did,
and I'm disgusted.'

89
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:30,340
'"You are a total pig."
Exclamation point.'

90
00:03:30,427 --> 00:03:32,255
"I used to think
you were special

91
00:03:32,342 --> 00:03:34,562
"but you're just a hypocrite
like the rest of them.

92
00:03:36,216 --> 00:03:39,001
"I can't believe I ever
thought you were cool.

93
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:41,438
'"I hate you."'

94
00:03:41,525 --> 00:03:42,787
[murmuring]

95
00:03:42,874 --> 00:03:46,574
'Who would write
such a letter? Hmm?'

96
00:03:46,661 --> 00:03:48,489
Clearly someone
who felt betrayed.

97
00:03:50,142 --> 00:03:52,623
A sensitive soul who now
feels deep remorse

98
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:55,060
for having spoken so harshly.

99
00:03:56,627 --> 00:03:59,717
Someone seated right here
in this room.

100
00:03:59,804 --> 00:04:01,197
Yes.

101
00:04:02,590 --> 00:04:04,809
[murmuring]

102
00:04:04,896 --> 00:04:07,029
You.

103
00:04:07,116 --> 00:04:08,509
While everyone else
was looking around

104
00:04:08,596 --> 00:04:09,814
trying to figure out
who wrote the letter

105
00:04:09,901 --> 00:04:11,425
'you weren't.'

106
00:04:11,512 --> 00:04:12,948
You knew who wrote
the letter

107
00:04:13,035 --> 00:04:14,689
because it was you.

108
00:04:14,776 --> 00:04:16,734
Isn't that right?

109
00:04:16,821 --> 00:04:19,302
I'm sorry.
I'm sorry.

110
00:04:19,389 --> 00:04:20,912
Oh, Lisbon, you're on.

111
00:04:20,999 --> 00:04:22,174
[murmuring]

112
00:04:22,262 --> 00:04:23,306
Wow.

113
00:04:23,393 --> 00:04:24,873
Hey! Hey, stop!

114
00:04:27,092 --> 00:04:28,529
Drama, huh?

115
00:04:30,313 --> 00:04:32,272
Anyone else have
anything to say?

116
00:04:33,447 --> 00:04:35,840
[theme music]

117
00:04:43,718 --> 00:04:44,980
Why'd you write
this letter, Rachel?

118
00:04:45,067 --> 00:04:47,199
[sniffling]
Mr. McT was my favorite teacher.

119
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:52,596
He always talked about
self-respect.

120
00:04:52,683 --> 00:04:56,905
'He wanted us girls to
value ourselves for our minds.'

121
00:04:56,992 --> 00:04:58,820
I-I mean, he was cool
with us.

122
00:04:58,907 --> 00:05:00,822
He didn't treat us
like idiot kids.

123
00:05:00,909 --> 00:05:02,389
Well, what changed?

124
00:05:02,476 --> 00:05:03,868
Three nights ago,
I was driving

125
00:05:03,955 --> 00:05:05,827
to the food bank
where I volunteer.

126
00:05:05,914 --> 00:05:07,698
It's in kind of
a sketchy neighborhood.

127
00:05:09,352 --> 00:05:12,877
And I saw Mr. McTierney
talking to, like, a hooker.

128
00:05:14,662 --> 00:05:16,925
And they were fighting.
Well, arguing.

129
00:05:17,012 --> 00:05:18,753
'And then she got
into his car.'

130
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:21,364
I mean..
He's a teacher.

131
00:05:22,583 --> 00:05:24,280
What did she look like?

132
00:05:24,367 --> 00:05:25,760
Here. I took a picture.

133
00:05:25,847 --> 00:05:26,978
[sniffles]

134
00:05:31,287 --> 00:05:33,550
[phone ringing]
Text me that picture

135
00:05:33,637 --> 00:05:35,857
and the address that you
saw her. Excuse me.

136
00:05:35,944 --> 00:05:37,075
Cho, what's up?

137
00:05:37,162 --> 00:05:38,860
[Kimball]
'Forensics finished up.'

138
00:05:38,947 --> 00:05:40,601
None of the school's bats
show traces of blood.

139
00:05:40,688 --> 00:05:42,124
And we found a baggie
of homemade pills

140
00:05:42,211 --> 00:05:45,257
in McTierney's car.
Looks like MDMA. Ecstasy.

141
00:05:45,345 --> 00:05:47,216
[Teresa]
'Turns out our victim
had a few secrets.'

142
00:05:47,303 --> 00:05:48,826
I've got a student here
who says she saw him

143
00:05:48,913 --> 00:05:50,654
in an argument
with a prostitute.

144
00:05:50,741 --> 00:05:52,787
I'll forward you a picture
of the hooker and the address.

145
00:05:52,874 --> 00:05:54,745
'You and Rigsby see
if you can track her down.'

146
00:05:54,832 --> 00:05:56,660
Got it.

147
00:05:56,747 --> 00:05:57,835
[beeping]

148
00:05:59,402 --> 00:06:00,360
Jane?

149
00:06:00,447 --> 00:06:01,796
Hey.

150
00:06:03,841 --> 00:06:05,930
Hmm? Ecstasy.

151
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:08,368
Interesting.

152
00:06:08,455 --> 00:06:10,892
[door opens]

153
00:06:10,979 --> 00:06:13,895
No, you go on.
I'm gonna stay here.

154
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:16,811
bye.

155
00:06:16,898 --> 00:06:19,291
I'm sorry. May I help you?

156
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:21,816
No. No help necessary.
Uh, you are?

157
00:06:21,903 --> 00:06:23,818
Ms. Austin.
Uh, drama teacher.

158
00:06:23,905 --> 00:06:25,733
Ah, I was just admiring
your set.

159
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:27,256
Uh, what play are you doing?

160
00:06:27,343 --> 00:06:28,518
"Hamlet."

161
00:06:28,605 --> 00:06:31,303
A modern interpretation,
as you can see.

162
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:33,262
"To die, to sleep.

163
00:06:33,349 --> 00:06:36,265
"To sleep,
perchance to dream.

164
00:06:36,352 --> 00:06:38,441
"Ay, there's the rub."

165
00:06:38,528 --> 00:06:40,312
That's right.

166
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:43,794
I'm, uh, worried
about Rachel.

167
00:06:45,230 --> 00:06:47,668
She's in the play.
She's really a good kid.

168
00:06:47,755 --> 00:06:49,017
Oh, she'll be fine

169
00:06:49,104 --> 00:06:50,410
unless she murdered
Mr. McT.

170
00:06:50,497 --> 00:06:52,934
- What do you think?
- No.

171
00:06:53,021 --> 00:06:55,110
No. Uh.. I can't imagine.

172
00:06:55,197 --> 00:06:56,372
Hmm.

173
00:06:57,460 --> 00:06:59,201
[laughing]

174
00:06:59,288 --> 00:07:00,811
[chuckles]

175
00:07:00,898 --> 00:07:03,684
Well, if you'll excuse me,
I have five minutes

176
00:07:03,771 --> 00:07:06,121
to get bad coffee
from the teachers' lounge.

177
00:07:06,208 --> 00:07:08,384
Mmm. Enjoy.

178
00:07:11,039 --> 00:07:12,170
[whooshing]

179
00:07:15,565 --> 00:07:16,697
[whooshing]

180
00:07:18,438 --> 00:07:19,569
[grunting]

181
00:07:21,266 --> 00:07:24,182
[instrumental music]

182
00:07:34,932 --> 00:07:36,064
[whooshing]

183
00:07:38,283 --> 00:07:39,894
[tapping]

184
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:41,112
En garde.

185
00:07:44,115 --> 00:07:46,596
Well, it's no fun
if you just give up.

186
00:07:48,990 --> 00:07:51,079
Are you one of the cops?

187
00:07:51,166 --> 00:07:53,560
Cop-adjacent, maybe.

188
00:07:53,647 --> 00:07:55,649
- Are you in the play?
- Yeah, I am.

189
00:07:55,736 --> 00:07:59,000
I'm Jeremiah.
I play the ghost.

190
00:07:59,087 --> 00:08:00,392
"I am thy father's spirit

191
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:03,265
"doomed for a certain term
to walk the night."

192
00:08:03,352 --> 00:08:05,789
Sounds cool when you say it.

193
00:08:05,876 --> 00:08:07,704
I'm not sure
I'm pulling it off.

194
00:08:09,184 --> 00:08:10,968
I only auditioned
to meet the girls.

195
00:08:11,055 --> 00:08:12,448
How's that going for you?

196
00:08:12,535 --> 00:08:14,015
Not good.

197
00:08:14,102 --> 00:08:15,538
[sighs]

198
00:08:15,625 --> 00:08:18,585
Well, Jeremiah, you seem
like a fellow in the know.

199
00:08:18,672 --> 00:08:21,152
Let me ask you this.

200
00:08:21,239 --> 00:08:23,764
Where could I buy
some drugs around here?

201
00:08:23,851 --> 00:08:27,550
- What?
- Ecstasy, preferably.

202
00:08:27,637 --> 00:08:29,509
I have no idea.

203
00:08:29,596 --> 00:08:31,206
Wh-why would you ask?

204
00:08:31,293 --> 00:08:33,948
Uh, I want to meet
the bad kids.

205
00:08:34,035 --> 00:08:37,038
You know, the kind that might
sell drugs to a teacher.

206
00:08:37,125 --> 00:08:40,171
Every school has them.
Where do they hang out?

207
00:08:40,258 --> 00:08:43,087
Um, I wo.. I wouldn't know
where they hang out.

208
00:08:43,174 --> 00:08:46,526
Mm, the bad kids
keep real low profile.

209
00:08:46,613 --> 00:08:48,571
Principal Snyder's
kind of a hard-ass.

210
00:08:48,658 --> 00:08:50,704
Hmm. You wouldn't know, huh?

211
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:53,707
Do you swear?

212
00:08:53,794 --> 00:08:56,361
Yeah. I swear.

213
00:08:58,842 --> 00:09:00,888
Well fought.

214
00:09:00,975 --> 00:09:02,846
Off with you.

215
00:09:06,589 --> 00:09:08,069
I know the due date's
just an estimate.

216
00:09:08,156 --> 00:09:10,680
But it.. I mean, it could be
today, right?

217
00:09:10,767 --> 00:09:13,640
Yea.. Just promise me that
you'll call or text.

218
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,078
Sorry. Sarah's freaking out.

219
00:09:17,165 --> 00:09:19,776
- How are you doing?
- Yeah, good. Solid.

220
00:09:19,863 --> 00:09:21,952
Definitely not about
to have a heart attack.

221
00:09:22,039 --> 00:09:23,737
Hey.

222
00:09:23,824 --> 00:09:26,217
Hey, now, back it up.
I'm just waiting on my laundry.

223
00:09:26,304 --> 00:09:28,219
Laundry? In that outfit?

224
00:09:28,306 --> 00:09:29,699
What are you,
a fashion critic?

225
00:09:29,786 --> 00:09:31,875
We're looking for this girl.

226
00:09:31,962 --> 00:09:33,355
'You seen her?'

227
00:09:33,442 --> 00:09:34,617
I don't know.

228
00:09:34,704 --> 00:09:36,358
I lost my glasses.

229
00:09:36,445 --> 00:09:38,360
Maybe if I had some money
for a new pair.

230
00:09:45,106 --> 00:09:46,934
Now I see.

231
00:09:47,021 --> 00:09:48,370
That's like White Chenille.

232
00:09:48,457 --> 00:09:49,850
Usually works
over on Fremont.

233
00:09:50,764 --> 00:09:51,895
Thank you.

234
00:09:51,982 --> 00:09:53,549
But she won't be out yet.

235
00:09:53,636 --> 00:09:55,986
You know where she stays?

236
00:09:56,073 --> 00:09:58,075
Come on, honey.
We've been over this.

237
00:10:01,557 --> 00:10:03,951
[indistinct chatter]

238
00:10:11,611 --> 00:10:12,742
Hmm.

239
00:10:15,136 --> 00:10:16,267
[door opens]

240
00:10:19,575 --> 00:10:22,404
Nice bathroom, Luis.
Uh, very clean.

241
00:10:22,491 --> 00:10:24,058
Sorry?

242
00:10:24,145 --> 00:10:28,149
I was just saying, bathroom
it's, uh, spotless.

243
00:10:28,236 --> 00:10:30,368
Where are all the bad kids?

244
00:10:30,455 --> 00:10:33,023
Bad kids? I-I don't know.

245
00:10:35,330 --> 00:10:36,853
Okay. Yeah.

246
00:10:38,028 --> 00:10:40,291
Nice chatting with you.

247
00:10:43,817 --> 00:10:46,210
[dramatic music]

248
00:10:49,474 --> 00:10:50,650
[rattling]

249
00:10:51,389 --> 00:10:53,435
Hello?

250
00:10:53,522 --> 00:10:54,654
[rattling]

251
00:10:59,702 --> 00:11:01,661
Uh, wait!

252
00:11:01,748 --> 00:11:02,879
[slamming]

253
00:11:07,405 --> 00:11:09,494
Hello, Mr. Jane.

254
00:11:09,581 --> 00:11:11,279
I'm sorry, if I'd have known
you wanted to come down here

255
00:11:11,366 --> 00:11:12,672
I would have had Luis
escort you.

256
00:11:12,759 --> 00:11:14,064
Perhaps you can help me out.

257
00:11:14,151 --> 00:11:15,152
I'm trying to find
the delinquents.

258
00:11:15,239 --> 00:11:16,676
Well, I..

259
00:11:16,763 --> 00:11:19,548
Found one myself,
or at least his shoes

260
00:11:19,635 --> 00:11:22,464
but he managed to get away.

261
00:11:22,551 --> 00:11:24,292
I don't think they're
gonna help you much.

262
00:11:24,379 --> 00:11:27,861
Mr. McTierney taught
mostly advanced classes.

263
00:11:27,948 --> 00:11:30,994
Yes, but it's the bad apples
that have the real dirt.

264
00:11:32,213 --> 00:11:33,605
And where are they?

265
00:11:33,693 --> 00:11:37,218
During class,
the kids are in class.

266
00:11:37,305 --> 00:11:39,611
It's a revolutionary
concept, I know.

267
00:11:39,699 --> 00:11:40,830
[bell ringing]

268
00:11:41,744 --> 00:11:43,528
Well.

269
00:11:43,615 --> 00:11:45,052
If you'll excuse me,
that's lunch.

270
00:11:45,139 --> 00:11:47,054
Speaking of which,
I'm a little peckish.

271
00:11:47,141 --> 00:11:49,709
Where's the cafeteria?

272
00:11:49,796 --> 00:11:50,927
[sighs]

273
00:11:52,842 --> 00:11:55,018
- Yes?
- Are you Chenille?

274
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:56,280
No. Who's Chenille?

275
00:11:56,367 --> 00:11:57,673
I'm Rigsby. This is Cho.
We're with CBI.

276
00:11:57,760 --> 00:11:59,153
Look, we know you go
by Chenille

277
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:02,591
and your real name's
Lindy Hayes.

278
00:12:02,678 --> 00:12:04,158
Seems like you got it all
figured out.

279
00:12:04,245 --> 00:12:05,812
What do you need me for?

280
00:12:05,899 --> 00:12:07,291
You were with this man,
Jack McTierney

281
00:12:07,378 --> 00:12:08,684
three nights ago?

282
00:12:09,859 --> 00:12:12,035
Yeah, so what?
I see him a lot.

283
00:12:12,122 --> 00:12:13,950
So he was a regular
of yours?

284
00:12:14,037 --> 00:12:16,170
No. He was
my English teacher.

285
00:12:17,519 --> 00:12:19,086
What is this about?

286
00:12:21,871 --> 00:12:24,265
[indistinct chattering]

287
00:12:32,273 --> 00:12:33,840
Hey. Excuse me.

288
00:12:33,927 --> 00:12:35,929
Can I borrow this?

289
00:12:39,584 --> 00:12:40,803
Oh, dear.

290
00:12:40,890 --> 00:12:44,198
You can have that back.

291
00:12:44,285 --> 00:12:45,765
[all gasps]

292
00:12:45,852 --> 00:12:48,289
Excuse me. I just need
a moment of your time

293
00:12:48,376 --> 00:12:50,291
then you can get back
to your lunch.

294
00:12:50,378 --> 00:12:52,597
I'd like you all
to raise your hands.

295
00:12:52,684 --> 00:12:54,382
All of you.

296
00:12:54,469 --> 00:12:56,558
It's official police business.

297
00:12:56,645 --> 00:12:58,342
Raise them high.

298
00:12:58,429 --> 00:13:00,649
Put your hand down
if you're on the Dean's list.

299
00:13:01,781 --> 00:13:04,566
[indistinct chatter]

300
00:13:04,653 --> 00:13:05,785
[Patrick]
'Good.'

301
00:13:07,830 --> 00:13:10,485
Okay, good.

302
00:13:10,572 --> 00:13:12,400
'Now put your hand down
if you're taking the SATs'

303
00:13:13,923 --> 00:13:16,360
or planning to take
the SATs.

304
00:13:18,014 --> 00:13:19,146
Okay then.

305
00:13:20,930 --> 00:13:24,891
Put your hand down if you've
never smoked a cigar..

306
00:13:24,978 --> 00:13:26,762
'Never mind.'

307
00:13:26,849 --> 00:13:29,199
Carry on.
Enjoy your meat loaf.

308
00:13:30,287 --> 00:13:31,419
[indistinct chatter]

309
00:13:32,507 --> 00:13:33,813
[birds chirping]

310
00:13:40,254 --> 00:13:41,864
[groaning]

311
00:13:41,951 --> 00:13:44,171
[male #1]
'Here get him.'

312
00:13:44,258 --> 00:13:46,651
[indistinct chattering]

313
00:13:50,351 --> 00:13:51,482
[grunting]

314
00:13:54,268 --> 00:13:55,660
[rustling]

315
00:13:55,747 --> 00:13:57,010
[male #2]
What was that?

316
00:13:57,097 --> 00:13:58,359
Ahh!

317
00:13:58,446 --> 00:14:00,665
Bad kids, at last!

318
00:14:00,752 --> 00:14:02,667
Uhh! No need to run.
Don't even think about it.

319
00:14:02,754 --> 00:14:04,539
The place is surrounded.

320
00:14:04,626 --> 00:14:07,150
Snipers on the rooftops.

321
00:14:07,237 --> 00:14:09,761
Just kidding. Who's running
the show here, you?

322
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:10,980
Or..

323
00:14:15,767 --> 00:14:17,552
You.

324
00:14:17,639 --> 00:14:19,771
What do you want?

325
00:14:19,859 --> 00:14:22,426
I'm Patrick.
What's your name?

326
00:14:22,513 --> 00:14:26,213
- Krista.
- Krista. I come in peace.

327
00:14:26,300 --> 00:14:27,910
So which of you crazy cats
is gonna help me

328
00:14:27,997 --> 00:14:31,522
solve McTierney's murder, huh?

329
00:14:31,609 --> 00:14:33,176
[Patrick]
Wow.

330
00:14:33,263 --> 00:14:34,656
[indistinct chattering]

331
00:14:34,743 --> 00:14:36,484
You really are bad.

332
00:14:37,833 --> 00:14:39,487
What do you wanna know?

333
00:14:39,574 --> 00:14:42,707
Well, while the rest of the kids
are stuck inside

334
00:14:42,794 --> 00:14:46,711
sharpening their pencils,
you're out here roaming free.

335
00:14:46,798 --> 00:14:49,845
I bet you know way more about
what's really going on, right?

336
00:14:49,932 --> 00:14:52,108
Like who's selling
the ecstasy, for starters.

337
00:14:53,544 --> 00:14:55,329
I wish I knew someone.
That would..

338
00:14:55,416 --> 00:14:57,331
That would definitely
be a popular kid.

339
00:14:57,418 --> 00:15:00,116
What about McTierney?
Did he have any secret vices?

340
00:15:01,944 --> 00:15:04,729
No. He was a square.

341
00:15:04,816 --> 00:15:06,688
Super clean-cut.

342
00:15:06,775 --> 00:15:08,995
Which is why it's so weird
he was tight with Loveland

343
00:15:09,082 --> 00:15:11,388
'cause Loveland's
a crazy drunk.

344
00:15:11,475 --> 00:15:13,608
Loveland?
The career counselor?

345
00:15:14,565 --> 00:15:15,653
- Yeah.
- Hmm.

346
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:17,873
See, a while back, um

347
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:19,701
Loveland confiscated
my friend's cell phone.

348
00:15:19,788 --> 00:15:21,050
Mm-hmm.

349
00:15:21,137 --> 00:15:22,399
So my friend broke
into his office

350
00:15:22,486 --> 00:15:23,661
and found like a million
of those little

351
00:15:23,748 --> 00:15:25,881
vodka airplane bottles,
empty.

352
00:15:27,665 --> 00:15:29,232
Well, thanks, Krista.

353
00:15:31,495 --> 00:15:32,714
Very much.

354
00:15:32,801 --> 00:15:34,281
You know, when you wake up
tomorrow morning

355
00:15:34,368 --> 00:15:35,804
and you look in the mirror,
you will see yourself

356
00:15:35,891 --> 00:15:37,458
as if for the very first time,
and you will decide

357
00:15:37,545 --> 00:15:39,982
'to never smoke
cigarettes again.'

358
00:15:42,245 --> 00:15:44,639
[instrumental music]

359
00:15:46,946 --> 00:15:48,860
Thank..
Thank you.

360
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:57,608
How did you know
Jack McTierney?

361
00:15:57,695 --> 00:15:59,784
He was my teacher.

362
00:15:59,871 --> 00:16:01,830
Like three years ago.

363
00:16:01,917 --> 00:16:03,571
Before I dropped out
of school.

364
00:16:05,051 --> 00:16:07,488
Back then,
he tried to help me

365
00:16:07,575 --> 00:16:10,621
with this problem I was having
with my stepfather.

366
00:16:10,708 --> 00:16:11,927
What was the problem?

367
00:16:13,929 --> 00:16:15,931
Take a wild guess.

368
00:16:16,018 --> 00:16:17,411
If your stepfather
was abusing you

369
00:16:17,498 --> 00:16:19,369
you can still have him
prosecuted.

370
00:16:19,456 --> 00:16:20,892
Whatever.

371
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:23,808
I ran away to the city.
Problem solved.

372
00:16:23,895 --> 00:16:28,639
But I guess Mr. McTierney
felt guilty, or something.

373
00:16:28,726 --> 00:16:32,165
'Cause ever since then,
he's been trying to rescue me.

374
00:16:32,252 --> 00:16:34,906
He freaked when I told him
I was turning tricks.

375
00:16:34,994 --> 00:16:36,386
What did he say?

376
00:16:36,473 --> 00:16:38,606
You know, that I could still
have a normal life.

377
00:16:38,693 --> 00:16:41,696
Stuff like that.

378
00:16:41,783 --> 00:16:46,179
Then afterward, he'd come
to see me like once a week

379
00:16:46,266 --> 00:16:48,311
buy me a cup of coffee

380
00:16:48,398 --> 00:16:50,487
talk to me about getting
my GED.

381
00:16:50,574 --> 00:16:53,099
But I didn't want to hear it.

382
00:16:53,186 --> 00:16:54,883
So we fought.

383
00:16:56,537 --> 00:16:58,974
We found drugs on McTierney.
Ecstasy.

384
00:17:00,541 --> 00:17:02,586
Couldn't have been
his stuff.

385
00:17:02,673 --> 00:17:04,284
He was a total
straight arrow.

386
00:17:04,371 --> 00:17:06,068
Was it yours?

387
00:17:06,155 --> 00:17:07,983
Ecstasy?

388
00:17:08,070 --> 00:17:10,377
That's for amateurs.

389
00:17:10,464 --> 00:17:12,161
Do you know if he was having
any problems at school?

390
00:17:14,207 --> 00:17:16,426
I don't know.

391
00:17:16,513 --> 00:17:18,907
I mean, he had a couple
other projects like me.

392
00:17:18,994 --> 00:17:21,040
I guess one of them
could have gone wrong.

393
00:17:21,127 --> 00:17:22,606
Projects?

394
00:17:22,693 --> 00:17:25,566
You know, kids he was
trying to help.

395
00:17:25,653 --> 00:17:27,611
You got any names?

396
00:17:27,698 --> 00:17:31,833
He tried to keep that stuff
pretty private.

397
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:35,228
But I heard him, arguing
on the phone with someone.

398
00:17:35,315 --> 00:17:37,230
'Martin.'

399
00:17:37,317 --> 00:17:40,233
Thanks.
You've been very helpful.

400
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:41,756
L-let me know if I can
do anything else.

401
00:17:43,410 --> 00:17:46,717
I want to help..
For Mr. McTierney.

402
00:17:46,804 --> 00:17:49,372
If you want to help,
you should do what he wanted.

403
00:17:49,459 --> 00:17:51,200
Go back to school.

404
00:17:53,768 --> 00:17:56,118
[indistinct chattering]

405
00:18:00,383 --> 00:18:02,516
[bell ringing]

406
00:18:09,349 --> 00:18:13,396
Mr. Loveland.
You got a minute?

407
00:18:13,483 --> 00:18:14,919
Of course. Come in.

408
00:18:15,006 --> 00:18:16,747
Yeah. A-anything
I can do to help.

409
00:18:16,834 --> 00:18:19,968
Well, in that case, why did you
punch Mr. McTierney?

410
00:18:22,057 --> 00:18:24,103
How did you know that?

411
00:18:24,190 --> 00:18:27,584
Oh. Well, I-I didn't,
but you just told me.

412
00:18:27,671 --> 00:18:29,630
Also, drunk people
aren't really known

413
00:18:29,717 --> 00:18:31,240
for their impulse control.

414
00:18:32,415 --> 00:18:34,374
Hey. Perfectly
understandable.

415
00:18:34,461 --> 00:18:36,637
You're working with
overachieving children all day.

416
00:18:36,724 --> 00:18:39,030
Who wouldn't want
to take the edge off? Huh?

417
00:18:39,118 --> 00:18:40,945
Look. I know what you
must be thinking

418
00:18:41,032 --> 00:18:44,253
but I had eight years sober.

419
00:18:44,340 --> 00:18:46,995
And, my wife left me

420
00:18:47,082 --> 00:18:49,867
and w-we got a new principal,
and, well, I started thinking

421
00:18:49,954 --> 00:18:53,262
that a drink might
be a good idea.

422
00:18:53,349 --> 00:18:57,179
About a month ago,
I, uh...tested that theory.

423
00:18:57,266 --> 00:18:59,790
Mmm. How'd that go?

424
00:18:59,877 --> 00:19:01,705
Okay. For a while.

425
00:19:03,446 --> 00:19:05,709
Then I started drinking
before work

426
00:19:05,796 --> 00:19:07,581
and I realized that
I was out of control.

427
00:19:07,668 --> 00:19:09,278
You confided in McTierney.

428
00:19:09,365 --> 00:19:10,975
I thought I could trust him.
I was wrong.

429
00:19:11,062 --> 00:19:12,890
He told the principal.

430
00:19:12,977 --> 00:19:14,544
Yeah.

431
00:19:14,631 --> 00:19:16,329
He screwed me royally.

432
00:19:16,416 --> 00:19:18,679
Doesn't seem like something
McTierney would do.

433
00:19:18,766 --> 00:19:21,290
I know. But when I asked Snyder
for some time off

434
00:19:21,377 --> 00:19:23,597
he knew about the drinking.

435
00:19:23,684 --> 00:19:27,078
He couldn't fire me for it,
but he could make my life hell.

436
00:19:31,431 --> 00:19:33,041
Okay.

437
00:19:33,128 --> 00:19:35,130
[Patrick clears throat]

438
00:19:35,217 --> 00:19:37,437
Uh, wh-wh-where were you

439
00:19:37,524 --> 00:19:40,179
when you, uh, uh, told
McTierney you relapsed?

440
00:19:40,266 --> 00:19:41,876
W-we were in the men's room.

441
00:19:41,963 --> 00:19:43,573
Was there anyone else there?

442
00:19:43,660 --> 00:19:45,096
You think I'd be talking
about that kind of stuff

443
00:19:45,184 --> 00:19:46,141
if there were?

444
00:19:46,228 --> 00:19:47,534
No, of course not.

445
00:19:48,665 --> 00:19:50,014
Thank you.

446
00:19:50,101 --> 00:19:51,233
[bell ringing]

447
00:19:56,020 --> 00:19:57,805
- I take it no baby yet.
- 'No.'

448
00:19:57,892 --> 00:19:59,546
Got anything
to distract me?

449
00:19:59,633 --> 00:20:01,548
Well, we finally got into
McTierney's computer

450
00:20:01,635 --> 00:20:03,114
and looked at his calendar.

451
00:20:03,202 --> 00:20:04,725
He had an advisory meeting
scheduled with a kid

452
00:20:04,812 --> 00:20:06,727
named Martin Klubock
the night he died.

453
00:20:06,814 --> 00:20:08,294
- Really?
- Yeah, but it's weird.

454
00:20:08,381 --> 00:20:10,339
Martin's an 'A' student,
and he has no history

455
00:20:10,426 --> 00:20:11,993
of disciplinary action
at school.

456
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:14,256
So why was he one of
Mr. McTierney's projects?

457
00:20:14,343 --> 00:20:15,736
Well..

458
00:20:15,823 --> 00:20:17,346
[sighs]
I looked into
his attendance records.

459
00:20:17,433 --> 00:20:19,392
He's been out sick
a bunch of times this year.

460
00:20:19,479 --> 00:20:21,089
And there was a trip
to the emergency room.

461
00:20:21,176 --> 00:20:22,438
A broken rib
and some stitches.

462
00:20:22,525 --> 00:20:24,092
You think it's
some kind of abuse?

463
00:20:24,179 --> 00:20:26,268
Could be, although his folks
don't have a record.

464
00:20:26,355 --> 00:20:28,183
Anyway, he called in sick
to school today.

465
00:20:28,270 --> 00:20:30,185
Yeah. Cho and I will
check it out. Gladly.

466
00:20:30,272 --> 00:20:33,275
That guilty creatures
sitting in a play

467
00:20:33,362 --> 00:20:36,974
have, by the, the
cunning of the scene

468
00:20:37,061 --> 00:20:39,586
so struck to the soul

469
00:20:39,673 --> 00:20:41,849
that presently..

470
00:20:41,936 --> 00:20:44,808
They have proclaimed
their malefactions

471
00:20:44,895 --> 00:20:46,506
their shortcomings.

472
00:20:46,593 --> 00:20:49,422
Mind if I borrow
some of this? Thank you.

473
00:20:49,509 --> 00:20:52,773
For murder,
though it have no tongue

474
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:59,083
will speak with most,
magnificent organ.

475
00:20:59,170 --> 00:21:01,390
I'll have these players
play something

476
00:21:01,477 --> 00:21:04,567
like the murder of my father
before mine uncle.

477
00:21:04,654 --> 00:21:05,873
'If he but blench..'

478
00:21:08,963 --> 00:21:11,618
I-I know my course.

479
00:21:11,705 --> 00:21:13,054
- Line?
- Billy!

480
00:21:13,141 --> 00:21:14,490
We have been
over this.

481
00:21:14,577 --> 00:21:16,362
You should have been
off book a week ago.

482
00:21:16,449 --> 00:21:18,668
The spirit that
I have seen may be a devil

483
00:21:18,755 --> 00:21:22,237
and the devil hath power
to assume a pleasing shape

484
00:21:22,324 --> 00:21:24,195
and so on and so forth.

485
00:21:25,196 --> 00:21:26,720
The play's the thing

486
00:21:26,807 --> 00:21:29,636
wherein I'll catch
the conscience of the king.

487
00:21:29,723 --> 00:21:31,942
[Ms. Austin]
'That was amazing!'

488
00:21:32,029 --> 00:21:33,901
Are you an actor, Mr. Jane?

489
00:21:33,988 --> 00:21:38,166
Well, I've, done a little
stage work here and there.

490
00:21:38,253 --> 00:21:40,124
Wow!

491
00:21:40,211 --> 00:21:42,301
I hope you guys
were paying attention.

492
00:21:42,388 --> 00:21:45,347
Billy, did you hear
the passion

493
00:21:45,434 --> 00:21:48,350
and clarity of his delivery,
the, the rhythm?

494
00:21:48,437 --> 00:21:50,134
Yeah. I heard.

495
00:21:50,221 --> 00:21:52,746
Okay. Everybody take five

496
00:21:52,833 --> 00:21:57,098
while I try to find out
what happened with the programs.

497
00:21:57,185 --> 00:21:59,622
Well, maybe Mr. Jane
could give us a few tips.

498
00:21:59,709 --> 00:22:01,320
I'm sure Mr. Jane doesn't
want to be bothered

499
00:22:01,407 --> 00:22:02,712
with our little play.

500
00:22:02,799 --> 00:22:05,106
- Oh, it's no bother.
- 'Well, then..'

501
00:22:05,193 --> 00:22:07,064
A brilliant idea.

502
00:22:07,151 --> 00:22:09,328
It's not every day we get
a visit from a professional.

503
00:22:09,415 --> 00:22:12,331
In that case, let's warm up with
a simple enunciation exercise

504
00:22:12,418 --> 00:22:13,636
shall we?

505
00:22:13,723 --> 00:22:16,291
Toy boat, ten times, fast.

506
00:22:16,378 --> 00:22:18,424
- 'Toy boat.'
- Toy boat. Toy boat.

507
00:22:18,511 --> 00:22:21,209
[in unison]
Toy boat. Toy boat. Toy boat.

508
00:22:21,296 --> 00:22:23,342
- Toy boat. Toy boat.
- 'Faster.'

509
00:22:25,126 --> 00:22:26,997
Jeremiah, you're not
projecting.

510
00:22:27,084 --> 00:22:28,521
Inhale from the bottom
of your stomach

511
00:22:28,608 --> 00:22:29,957
not from the top
of your throat.

512
00:22:30,044 --> 00:22:32,394
Deep breaths.

513
00:22:32,481 --> 00:22:35,179
Ay, that incestuous,
that adulterate beast--

514
00:22:35,266 --> 00:22:36,703
More voice, more voice.

515
00:22:36,790 --> 00:22:39,053
Ay, that incestuous,
that adulterate beast!

516
00:22:39,140 --> 00:22:41,055
[Ms. Austin]
'Yes!'

517
00:22:41,142 --> 00:22:44,406
Yes, bravo, Jeremiah!

518
00:22:45,451 --> 00:22:47,409
You are good, Mr. Jane.

519
00:22:47,496 --> 00:22:50,281
Any notes for Billy,
our Hamlet?

520
00:22:50,369 --> 00:22:52,283
Well, remember, Billy,
never break character

521
00:22:52,371 --> 00:22:53,546
even if you forget
your lines.

522
00:22:53,633 --> 00:22:54,851
I mean, the audience
will never know

523
00:22:54,938 --> 00:22:56,331
as long as you keep going.

524
00:22:56,418 --> 00:22:58,638
Thank you. I have been
telling him that.

525
00:22:58,725 --> 00:23:01,641
And Hamlet's not as pleased
with himself as you are

526
00:23:01,728 --> 00:23:04,208
so, uh, dial that back
a touch.

527
00:23:04,295 --> 00:23:06,863
As for you, Ms. Austin

528
00:23:06,950 --> 00:23:08,517
you have, uh..

529
00:23:09,910 --> 00:23:12,391
something on your...coat.

530
00:23:12,478 --> 00:23:14,958
'Huh. A leaf.'

531
00:23:16,525 --> 00:23:17,787
There. All set.

532
00:23:17,874 --> 00:23:21,138
Oh. Thank you, Mr. Jane.

533
00:23:21,225 --> 00:23:22,879
You should come back tomorrow
and see the play.

534
00:23:22,966 --> 00:23:25,316
I'm sure all the kinks
will be worked out by then.

535
00:23:25,404 --> 00:23:28,145
Yeah. Break a leg.

536
00:23:28,232 --> 00:23:29,973
- Martin Klubock?
- Uh, yes.

537
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:31,453
I'm Agent Rigsby.
This is Agent Cho.

538
00:23:31,540 --> 00:23:33,237
We're with CBI.
Your parents home?

539
00:23:33,324 --> 00:23:34,891
Uh, no.

540
00:23:34,978 --> 00:23:37,154
No, they're at a sales
convention in Tucson.

541
00:23:37,241 --> 00:23:38,634
But they'll be back
Saturday.

542
00:23:38,721 --> 00:23:41,071
We need to talk to you
about Mr. McTierney.

543
00:23:41,158 --> 00:23:43,334
Yeah. Of course.

544
00:23:43,422 --> 00:23:45,032
You got something burning?

545
00:23:45,119 --> 00:23:47,643
Uh, no. No, it's fine.
It's just some soup.

546
00:23:49,384 --> 00:23:51,168
You had a meeting
with Mr. McTierney last night

547
00:23:51,255 --> 00:23:52,561
an hour before he died.

548
00:23:52,648 --> 00:23:54,563
Uh, I was supposed
to meet with him.

549
00:23:54,650 --> 00:23:56,260
Uh, but I canceled.

550
00:23:56,347 --> 00:23:57,914
I'm coming down
with something.

551
00:23:58,001 --> 00:23:59,394
Really, you got something
burning. I should check it out.

552
00:23:59,481 --> 00:24:02,266
- Uh, no, it's fine.
- No, come on! Hey! Hey!

553
00:24:07,924 --> 00:24:09,926
[hissing]
Hey!

554
00:24:10,013 --> 00:24:11,624
What the hell
are you doing?

555
00:24:11,711 --> 00:24:13,060
[bubbling]

556
00:24:16,367 --> 00:24:17,499
Making ecstasy.

557
00:24:19,675 --> 00:24:20,807
[bell ringing]

558
00:24:27,814 --> 00:24:30,686
[instrumental music]

559
00:24:46,876 --> 00:24:48,312
Et voila.

560
00:24:52,882 --> 00:24:55,450
[indistinct chattering]

561
00:24:55,537 --> 00:24:57,365
[woman over PA]
'Patrick Jane, please report'

562
00:24:57,452 --> 00:24:59,759
'to the principal's office
immediately.'

563
00:24:59,846 --> 00:25:03,197
'I repeat, Patrick Jane,
to the principal's office.'

564
00:25:06,635 --> 00:25:08,289
[door opens]

565
00:25:08,376 --> 00:25:10,421
There he is. The principal
will be in shortly.

566
00:25:10,509 --> 00:25:11,640
[sighs]

567
00:25:13,076 --> 00:25:14,817
What did you do?

568
00:25:15,992 --> 00:25:17,646
Nothing.

569
00:25:17,733 --> 00:25:19,256
11 years in catholic school

570
00:25:19,343 --> 00:25:21,694
and I never got sent to
the principal's office once.

571
00:25:21,781 --> 00:25:24,000
Well, that's because you're
a boring goody-two-shoes.

572
00:25:24,087 --> 00:25:25,741
I didn't say I never did
anything bad.

573
00:25:25,828 --> 00:25:28,527
- I just never got caught.
- Ahh. Touche.

574
00:25:30,137 --> 00:25:31,268
[door opens]

575
00:25:35,969 --> 00:25:37,100
[sighs]

576
00:25:41,540 --> 00:25:42,671
[sniffing]

577
00:25:47,328 --> 00:25:51,375
It has come to my attention

578
00:25:51,462 --> 00:25:54,248
'that Mr. Jane here'

579
00:25:54,335 --> 00:25:55,989
performed an act of vandalism

580
00:25:56,076 --> 00:25:58,644
in the second floor
boys restroom.

581
00:26:00,384 --> 00:26:02,691
A vicious, personal attack
on me.

582
00:26:04,345 --> 00:26:05,520
Jane?

583
00:26:07,740 --> 00:26:09,872
It's true.

584
00:26:09,959 --> 00:26:13,223
Sir, on behalf of the CBI,
I apologize.

585
00:26:14,921 --> 00:26:16,575
I appreciate that.

586
00:26:18,620 --> 00:26:19,969
Mr. Jane?

587
00:26:23,233 --> 00:26:24,408
Jane!

588
00:26:29,675 --> 00:26:32,808
I'm sorry for the senseless
act of graffiti.

589
00:26:34,462 --> 00:26:36,116
That's all.

590
00:26:37,117 --> 00:26:38,771
Very well.

591
00:26:40,642 --> 00:26:42,165
I will send a bill to CBI

592
00:26:42,252 --> 00:26:44,037
which will cover the cost
of cleaning

593
00:26:44,124 --> 00:26:46,256
and I would ask
that as we move forward

594
00:26:46,343 --> 00:26:48,911
with this investigation

595
00:26:48,998 --> 00:26:51,914
it is conducted without
Mr. Jane on site.

596
00:26:52,001 --> 00:26:53,655
Are we agreed?

597
00:26:53,742 --> 00:26:56,876
How'd you know it was me
that graffiti'd the bathroom?

598
00:26:56,963 --> 00:26:58,529
A student told me.

599
00:26:58,617 --> 00:27:01,358
Well, that's strange,
'cause it was during class

600
00:27:01,445 --> 00:27:03,578
and there was no one else
in the bathroom

601
00:27:03,665 --> 00:27:05,058
'cause I checked.

602
00:27:05,145 --> 00:27:07,016
Well, somebody saw something,
didn't they?

603
00:27:07,103 --> 00:27:08,322
Mmm.

604
00:27:09,323 --> 00:27:10,672
You saw something

605
00:27:10,759 --> 00:27:13,414
on your unauthorized
security cameras.

606
00:27:14,894 --> 00:27:16,156
What?

607
00:27:16,243 --> 00:27:17,897
He has unauthorized
security cameras

608
00:27:17,984 --> 00:27:19,812
hidden in the bathrooms.

609
00:27:19,899 --> 00:27:22,031
It's a little creepy, huh?

610
00:27:24,164 --> 00:27:25,774
'I don't know where
you are getting'

611
00:27:25,861 --> 00:27:28,081
your information,
but it is simply not true!

612
00:27:28,168 --> 00:27:30,257
Oh! Oh!

613
00:27:30,344 --> 00:27:31,432
[Patrick]
'Hello!'

614
00:27:31,519 --> 00:27:33,303
Pervy perv perv!

615
00:27:33,390 --> 00:27:35,175
Secret cameras?
That is profoundly illegal.

616
00:27:35,262 --> 00:27:37,699
No. Listen..

617
00:27:37,786 --> 00:27:40,006
Listen to me.

618
00:27:40,093 --> 00:27:44,924
These cameras are for
disciplinary purposes only.

619
00:27:45,011 --> 00:27:46,795
Then why didn't you
tell us?

620
00:27:46,882 --> 00:27:49,058
Because, as you say

621
00:27:49,145 --> 00:27:52,583
strictly speaking,
they are not 100% legal.

622
00:27:52,671 --> 00:27:54,629
- Mm-hmm.
- 'Principal Snyder'

623
00:27:54,716 --> 00:27:56,979
if you're withholding evidence
from this investigation

624
00:27:57,066 --> 00:27:58,677
we've got
a serious problem.

625
00:27:58,764 --> 00:28:01,201
'And the PTA
and the ACLU.'

626
00:28:01,288 --> 00:28:02,898
And all those other acronyms

627
00:28:02,985 --> 00:28:05,684
they're gonna have a host
of problems with you, pal.

628
00:28:05,771 --> 00:28:08,208
I wouldn't think
of withholding evidence.

629
00:28:08,295 --> 00:28:10,645
I'm not an idiot.

630
00:28:10,732 --> 00:28:13,082
I looked through the footage
the night of the murder

631
00:28:13,169 --> 00:28:15,215
and I don't think
there is anything there.

632
00:28:15,302 --> 00:28:17,608
Frankly, I don't care
what you think.

633
00:28:17,696 --> 00:28:20,481
'I want all the footage now.'

634
00:28:20,568 --> 00:28:22,352
Alright. I understand.

635
00:28:23,745 --> 00:28:25,355
[breathing heavily]

636
00:28:25,442 --> 00:28:28,445
Agent Lisbon,
could I ask that we

637
00:28:28,532 --> 00:28:30,230
keep this discreet?

638
00:28:30,317 --> 00:28:31,492
No.

639
00:28:34,147 --> 00:28:35,409
Martin..

640
00:28:37,193 --> 00:28:39,674
...you've been
manufacturing ecstasy

641
00:28:39,761 --> 00:28:41,067
a schedule I,
narcotic.

642
00:28:42,721 --> 00:28:45,332
You're 16 years old.

643
00:28:45,419 --> 00:28:46,942
You can be tried
as an adult

644
00:28:47,029 --> 00:28:48,509
or you can be tried
as a minor.

645
00:28:48,596 --> 00:28:52,034
Believe me, you don't want
to be tried as an adult.

646
00:28:52,121 --> 00:28:55,037
You lied about meeting
Mr. McTierney the night he died.

647
00:28:55,995 --> 00:28:57,344
Okay. I met him.

648
00:28:57,431 --> 00:28:58,693
And he confiscated
your pills?

649
00:28:58,780 --> 00:29:00,869
That's why we found
the drugs on him, right?

650
00:29:00,956 --> 00:29:03,742
Yeah. But he didn't know
I was making them. I swear.

651
00:29:05,047 --> 00:29:06,135
I told him
I found the pills

652
00:29:06,222 --> 00:29:07,484
and he believed me.

653
00:29:07,571 --> 00:29:10,052
What about your parents?
They must have known

654
00:29:10,139 --> 00:29:11,750
that there was
a drug lab in their basement.

655
00:29:11,837 --> 00:29:14,274
Uh, they had no idea
about the drugs.

656
00:29:14,361 --> 00:29:16,189
'I told them it was for
my science fair project.'

657
00:29:16,276 --> 00:29:17,843
Okay.

658
00:29:17,930 --> 00:29:20,454
So why were you meeting
Mr. McTierney?

659
00:29:22,064 --> 00:29:25,676
I had been having
some, trouble,and..

660
00:29:25,764 --> 00:29:27,504
'...he was trying to help me.'

661
00:29:29,289 --> 00:29:32,248
Something to do with your,
uh, trip to the emergency room?

662
00:29:34,163 --> 00:29:35,295
Yeah.

663
00:29:36,165 --> 00:29:37,688
Martin..

664
00:29:42,389 --> 00:29:44,565
Are you getting abused
at home?

665
00:29:44,652 --> 00:29:47,394
No. No way.

666
00:29:47,481 --> 00:29:49,091
My parents aren't around
enough to abuse me.

667
00:29:49,178 --> 00:29:51,267
So you're getting bullied
at school?

668
00:29:53,443 --> 00:29:55,532
Not really.

669
00:29:55,619 --> 00:29:58,927
I mean, kids can be morons,
but...it's not that bad.

670
00:29:59,014 --> 00:30:00,450
I went to high school.

671
00:30:00,537 --> 00:30:02,365
They eat kids
like you for breakfast.

672
00:30:02,452 --> 00:30:05,716
That's what Mr. McTierney
was helping you with, right?

673
00:30:08,502 --> 00:30:09,938
Whatever.

674
00:30:10,025 --> 00:30:12,462
He tried,
but what was he gonna do?

675
00:30:12,549 --> 00:30:14,682
'He couldn't be there
24/7.'

676
00:30:14,769 --> 00:30:17,946
And what kind of wuss needs a
teacher to protect him, anyways?

677
00:30:18,033 --> 00:30:19,687
It just made things worse.

678
00:30:19,774 --> 00:30:21,515
So you decided
to become a drug dealer.

679
00:30:21,602 --> 00:30:23,125
[scoffs]

680
00:30:23,212 --> 00:30:25,214
I just wanted to be popular.

681
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:26,737
I thought if I became
the cool drug guy

682
00:30:26,825 --> 00:30:28,478
then everything
would be different.

683
00:30:28,565 --> 00:30:29,697
I wasn't even
gonna sell the E

684
00:30:29,784 --> 00:30:31,830
just give it away.

685
00:30:31,917 --> 00:30:33,657
I know that's crazy, but..

686
00:30:34,833 --> 00:30:38,053
I would do anything.

687
00:30:38,140 --> 00:30:39,750
You know what?

688
00:30:39,838 --> 00:30:41,622
I got my ass kicked
in high school.

689
00:30:41,709 --> 00:30:43,537
- Yeah, right.
- No, seriously.

690
00:30:43,624 --> 00:30:45,582
I was 5'4"
till my senior year.

691
00:30:45,669 --> 00:30:47,758
Then I grew a foot.
Things change.

692
00:30:49,064 --> 00:30:50,587
That would be so awesome.

693
00:30:50,674 --> 00:30:52,720
[phone vibrating]
Wait a second.

694
00:30:54,330 --> 00:30:56,028
Hey. What's up?

695
00:30:56,115 --> 00:30:58,552
Your.. Your what broke?

696
00:30:58,639 --> 00:31:01,642
Oh! Oh, oh! Uh, yeah, yeah.
Um, I'm on my way.

697
00:31:01,729 --> 00:31:03,078
'Uh, don't move!'

698
00:31:10,520 --> 00:31:11,957
- Hey.
- Hey.

699
00:31:12,044 --> 00:31:13,045
- Everything okay?
- Her water broke.

700
00:31:13,132 --> 00:31:14,568
That's a good thing,
isn't it?

701
00:31:14,655 --> 00:31:16,570
Yeah. It's, uh...it's happening.
Can you tell Lisbon?

702
00:31:16,657 --> 00:31:18,224
- Yeah, I got you covered.
- Thank you.

703
00:31:18,311 --> 00:31:19,442
Good luck.

704
00:31:20,661 --> 00:31:21,836
[crickets chirping]

705
00:31:23,838 --> 00:31:26,797
[Grace sighs]
'We've watched 60 hours
worth of footage.'

706
00:31:26,885 --> 00:31:28,669
I think it's a dead end.

707
00:31:28,756 --> 00:31:30,671
Patience, grasshopper.

708
00:31:31,585 --> 00:31:33,152
I am being patient.

709
00:31:36,372 --> 00:31:37,852
There.

710
00:31:37,939 --> 00:31:39,549
Back that up.

711
00:31:39,636 --> 00:31:40,811
Okay.

712
00:31:45,033 --> 00:31:46,208
Can you.. Can you
grow that?

713
00:31:46,295 --> 00:31:48,732
Can you.. Can you
bigify it?

714
00:31:48,819 --> 00:31:50,343
I can do a digital zoom.

715
00:31:51,431 --> 00:31:52,562
[Patrick]
'Good.'

716
00:31:54,303 --> 00:31:57,002
- 'You know that kid?'
- 'Our Hamlet.'

717
00:31:58,264 --> 00:31:59,787
Well, that's interesting.

718
00:32:01,528 --> 00:32:03,747
That's well, that's
very interesting.

719
00:32:06,315 --> 00:32:07,838
What's interesting?

720
00:32:09,623 --> 00:32:10,798
I just talked to the DA.

721
00:32:10,885 --> 00:32:12,582
They're gonna go easy
on Martin

722
00:32:12,669 --> 00:32:14,062
but they don't want to pursue
Principal Snyder

723
00:32:14,149 --> 00:32:16,282
on the illegal cameras,
no upside.

724
00:32:16,369 --> 00:32:18,327
Well, that's a shame.
Let's get your jacket on.

725
00:32:18,414 --> 00:32:20,155
- We have to go.
- Where are we going?

726
00:32:20,242 --> 00:32:21,765
Back to school.

727
00:32:21,852 --> 00:32:24,246
[instrumental music]

728
00:32:26,161 --> 00:32:28,642
[indistinct chatter]

729
00:32:31,993 --> 00:32:34,604
Excellent. A full house.
Make sure you get a good seat.

730
00:32:34,691 --> 00:32:36,302
You're not coming with me?

731
00:32:36,389 --> 00:32:39,653
Well, I-I just have to make
a quick stop first.

732
00:32:41,481 --> 00:32:42,656
[sighs]

733
00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:50,969
[rattling]

734
00:32:56,061 --> 00:33:00,152
[Ms. Austin]
'Can I have my thespians
stage left, please?'

735
00:33:00,239 --> 00:33:02,589
'Time for our pre-show circle.
Looking good.'

736
00:33:02,676 --> 00:33:05,200
'Wonderful. Wonderful.'

737
00:33:05,287 --> 00:33:06,723
Five minutes.

738
00:33:06,810 --> 00:33:08,595
Oh. Hello, Mr. Jane.

739
00:33:08,682 --> 00:33:09,900
How's it going back here?

740
00:33:09,988 --> 00:33:11,728
Great. Oh, Sarah.

741
00:33:11,815 --> 00:33:14,253
Time to get
to the light board.

742
00:33:14,340 --> 00:33:16,124
'Lexi, please tuck in
that shirt.'

743
00:33:16,211 --> 00:33:18,039
I-I'm sorry, Mr. Jane.
I have to go.

744
00:33:18,126 --> 00:33:20,781
- It is absolute chaos.
- Of course. Of course.

745
00:33:22,130 --> 00:33:24,306
Jeremiah.
How are you feeling?

746
00:33:24,393 --> 00:33:26,482
You look a little green
around the gills.

747
00:33:26,569 --> 00:33:28,832
Uh.. I'm a little nervous.

748
00:33:28,919 --> 00:33:30,399
Well, that's to be expected.

749
00:33:30,486 --> 00:33:32,227
Why don't I give you
a little breathing exercise

750
00:33:32,314 --> 00:33:33,533
just to, to ground you?

751
00:33:33,620 --> 00:33:34,838
Yeah, sure, anything.

752
00:33:34,925 --> 00:33:38,103
Belly to the lips.
Mm, mm, mm.

753
00:33:38,190 --> 00:33:41,019
- Belly to the lips. Mm..
- Mm.

754
00:33:41,106 --> 00:33:42,455
[ululating]

755
00:33:42,542 --> 00:33:43,673
[both ululating]

756
00:33:44,805 --> 00:33:46,154
Okay. Short, quick breaths.

757
00:33:50,550 --> 00:33:51,812
Yeah. More.

758
00:33:52,682 --> 00:33:54,162
short and quick.

759
00:33:54,249 --> 00:33:56,469
Okay.. Faster.

760
00:33:56,556 --> 00:33:58,123
Now big, deep, long..

761
00:33:58,210 --> 00:34:00,734
Lots of oxygen.
Lots of oxygen.

762
00:34:00,821 --> 00:34:02,562
Oh.. Oh.

763
00:34:03,737 --> 00:34:05,695
There you go.

764
00:34:05,782 --> 00:34:07,784
Look how calm
you are now.

765
00:34:08,698 --> 00:34:10,352
Yes, yes.

766
00:34:13,051 --> 00:34:15,444
[indistinct chattering]

767
00:34:18,273 --> 00:34:20,928
um, there's someone
sitting there.

768
00:34:27,978 --> 00:34:29,110
Hi.

769
00:34:29,197 --> 00:34:30,851
Oh, my God!

770
00:34:30,938 --> 00:34:32,287
Should we call
an ambulance?

771
00:34:32,374 --> 00:34:34,811
No. No, no.
I think I'm okay.

772
00:34:34,898 --> 00:34:36,117
I-I don't know
what happened.

773
00:34:36,204 --> 00:34:38,032
You hyperventilated.
Silly boy.

774
00:34:38,119 --> 00:34:39,729
I think he'll be fine.

775
00:34:39,816 --> 00:34:41,470
I don't know that he'll be
able to perform tonight..

776
00:34:41,557 --> 00:34:43,646
But you do have
an understudy, don't you?

777
00:34:43,733 --> 00:34:46,823
We have oneunderstudy,
and he's already being used

778
00:34:46,910 --> 00:34:48,390
because Laertes has mono.

779
00:34:48,477 --> 00:34:51,480
- Oh.
- This is just a disaster.

780
00:34:51,567 --> 00:34:53,569
There's no way we can
go on without a ghost.

781
00:34:53,656 --> 00:34:55,528
Oh, we have to.
We've worked so hard.

782
00:34:55,615 --> 00:34:58,313
Can't we replace him?
Jeremiah wasn't even that good.

783
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:00,315
Billy, don't say that.

784
00:35:00,402 --> 00:35:02,622
Who could we possibly get
to stand in for him?

785
00:35:02,709 --> 00:35:04,319
I don't know.
It's such an important role.

786
00:35:04,406 --> 00:35:06,016
- Stay down. Stay down.
- I know!

787
00:35:06,104 --> 00:35:07,627
Oh, you could do it,
Mr. Jane.

788
00:35:07,714 --> 00:35:08,715
You already know the lines,
don't you?

789
00:35:08,802 --> 00:35:10,847
Well, I-I-I couldn't possibly.

790
00:35:10,934 --> 00:35:12,849
Oh, please!
We're screwed if you don't.

791
00:35:12,936 --> 00:35:14,721
Yeah, come on, dude!

792
00:35:15,852 --> 00:35:17,158
What do you say,
Ms. Austin?

793
00:35:17,245 --> 00:35:20,248
I think, it's a brilliant idea.

794
00:35:21,380 --> 00:35:22,729
Good job, Rachel.

795
00:35:22,816 --> 00:35:24,513
Okay then. Well..

796
00:35:24,600 --> 00:35:26,211
The show must go on.

797
00:35:26,298 --> 00:35:28,735
Thank you. And you can
stay in what you're wearing.

798
00:35:28,822 --> 00:35:30,215
- It's perfect.
- Uh, uh..

799
00:35:30,302 --> 00:35:31,564
[female #1]
'Two minutes!'

800
00:35:31,651 --> 00:35:33,305
Overture and beginners!

801
00:35:34,958 --> 00:35:36,482
Stay down.

802
00:35:41,226 --> 00:35:43,619
[indistinct chattering]

803
00:35:47,841 --> 00:35:49,016
[mouthing]

804
00:35:58,156 --> 00:35:59,505
Have after. To what
issue will this come?

805
00:35:59,592 --> 00:36:01,811
Something is rotten
in the state of Denmark.

806
00:36:01,898 --> 00:36:03,291
[male #3]
'Heaven will direct it.'

807
00:36:03,378 --> 00:36:05,902
[male #4]
'Nay, let's follow him.'

808
00:36:05,989 --> 00:36:08,078
Alright, Mr. Jane,
Billy, scene five.

809
00:36:08,166 --> 00:36:09,428
You are on.

810
00:36:15,999 --> 00:36:18,524
Wither wilt thou lead me?

811
00:36:18,611 --> 00:36:19,873
'Speak. I'll go no further.'

812
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:21,483
- 'Mark me.'
- 'I will.'

813
00:36:21,570 --> 00:36:24,269
My hour is almost come

814
00:36:24,356 --> 00:36:27,620
when I to sulphurous
and tormenting flames

815
00:36:27,707 --> 00:36:29,535
'must render myself up.'

816
00:36:29,622 --> 00:36:32,538
- 'Alas, poor ghost.'
- 'Pity me not.'

817
00:36:32,625 --> 00:36:36,063
Lend thy serious hearing
to what I shall unfold.

818
00:36:37,195 --> 00:36:40,850
A tale of murder most foul.

819
00:36:42,504 --> 00:36:44,506
The murder of Mr. McTierney!

820
00:36:45,725 --> 00:36:47,161
[audience murmuring]

821
00:36:47,248 --> 00:36:49,032
Uh, uh, su-surely..

822
00:36:49,119 --> 00:36:51,818
th-thou means the murder
of my father, the king.

823
00:36:51,905 --> 00:36:54,124
A murder born of lust
in the woods

824
00:36:54,212 --> 00:36:56,910
next to the baseball field.

825
00:36:56,997 --> 00:37:00,696
N-nay! Thou means
the woods in Denmark! Right?

826
00:37:00,783 --> 00:37:04,396
A murder born of unnatural lust
between woman and boy.

827
00:37:06,267 --> 00:37:07,399
[audience murmuring]

828
00:37:08,226 --> 00:37:10,010
Um.. uh, line?

829
00:37:11,838 --> 00:37:14,362
A murder committed by you
with this baseball bat.

830
00:37:15,842 --> 00:37:17,452
N-no! I-it wasn't me!

831
00:37:17,539 --> 00:37:19,149
- No?
- It was her!

832
00:37:21,369 --> 00:37:23,589
Whoa, whoa!
Lisbon, we have a runner!

833
00:37:25,591 --> 00:37:27,723
Whoa!

834
00:37:27,810 --> 00:37:30,160
Hey.

835
00:37:30,248 --> 00:37:31,205
[grunting]

836
00:37:31,292 --> 00:37:32,424
Whoa.

837
00:37:36,166 --> 00:37:37,864
[chuckles]

838
00:37:37,951 --> 00:37:39,953
Well, thank you.
You've been a lovely audience.

839
00:37:40,040 --> 00:37:42,434
Sorry we had to close..
early

840
00:37:42,521 --> 00:37:45,437
but you get the general idea.
Pretty much everyone dies

841
00:37:45,524 --> 00:37:47,047
poisoning, stabbing.

842
00:37:47,134 --> 00:37:49,658
Yeah, it's all very sad.
The Scandinavians, huh?

843
00:37:49,745 --> 00:37:51,138
Gloomy bunch.

844
00:37:51,225 --> 00:37:53,183
Oh, and by the way, uh

845
00:37:53,271 --> 00:37:55,795
don't do anything naughty
in the bathrooms.

846
00:37:55,882 --> 00:37:58,624
Principal Snyder has
hidden cameras in there.

847
00:37:58,711 --> 00:38:00,452
'Illegal? Yes.'

848
00:38:00,539 --> 00:38:03,672
Immoral? You decide.

849
00:38:03,759 --> 00:38:04,891
[audience jeering]

850
00:38:04,978 --> 00:38:06,458
Goodnight.

851
00:38:06,545 --> 00:38:08,677
We found the bat
in your house.

852
00:38:08,764 --> 00:38:12,202
Ms. Austin is probably
blaming it all on you.

853
00:38:12,290 --> 00:38:14,857
So help yourself out.
Talk to me.

854
00:38:19,558 --> 00:38:21,124
She's 35. You're 16.

855
00:38:21,211 --> 00:38:23,431
That's statutory rape.

856
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:26,608
So tell me what happened.

857
00:38:26,695 --> 00:38:27,827
[gasps]

858
00:38:30,090 --> 00:38:33,354
It started last semester
in her acting class.

859
00:38:33,441 --> 00:38:36,923
She was, like, passionate
about everything, you know?

860
00:38:37,010 --> 00:38:38,925
- Mm-hmm.
- It was exciting.

861
00:38:39,012 --> 00:38:41,710
And...she told me
I was talented.

862
00:38:43,408 --> 00:38:45,410
And asked me to audition
for the play.

863
00:38:47,803 --> 00:38:51,329
[Billy]
We started hooking up
after rehearsals.

864
00:38:54,157 --> 00:38:58,379
And then Mr. McTierney saw us when he was jogging that night.

865
00:39:02,209 --> 00:39:05,038
He said he was gonna
go to Principal Snyder.

866
00:39:05,125 --> 00:39:07,432
He said she was
gonna go to jail.

867
00:39:09,434 --> 00:39:12,480
She asked me to go
after him and stop him.

868
00:39:13,916 --> 00:39:16,136
She just wanted to talk
to him, she said

869
00:39:16,223 --> 00:39:18,312
to change his mind.

870
00:39:18,399 --> 00:39:20,488
I-I followed him
through the woods

871
00:39:20,575 --> 00:39:22,664
and out onto
the baseball field.

872
00:39:22,751 --> 00:39:24,231
I begged him not to tell.

873
00:39:24,318 --> 00:39:26,538
I didn't want her
to get in trouble.

874
00:39:29,715 --> 00:39:30,846
[grunting]

875
00:39:32,195 --> 00:39:34,589
[dramatic music]

876
00:39:38,941 --> 00:39:41,988
I know what I did
to Mr. McTierney

877
00:39:42,075 --> 00:39:43,816
is unforgivable

878
00:39:43,903 --> 00:39:48,037
and I wish to God
I could undo it

879
00:39:48,124 --> 00:39:50,257
but you have to understand.

880
00:39:50,344 --> 00:39:53,478
What Billy and I have
is special.

881
00:39:53,565 --> 00:39:54,827
It's real.

882
00:39:55,828 --> 00:39:57,786
I'm not some pervert.

883
00:39:58,918 --> 00:40:02,312
I love him,
and he loves me.

884
00:40:04,837 --> 00:40:07,709
Is that wrong?

885
00:40:07,796 --> 00:40:08,928
Yes.

886
00:40:11,539 --> 00:40:12,671
[sighs]

887
00:40:24,683 --> 00:40:27,468
Lindy, hi.
What are you doing here?

888
00:40:27,555 --> 00:40:29,296
I just wanted to say
thank you

889
00:40:29,383 --> 00:40:31,646
For figuring out what happened
to Mr. McTierney.

890
00:40:31,733 --> 00:40:34,432
Ohh...you're welcome.

891
00:40:34,519 --> 00:40:36,738
And also..

892
00:40:36,825 --> 00:40:39,959
I think I want to do
what he said.

893
00:40:40,046 --> 00:40:41,961
You know,
get out of the life.

894
00:40:42,048 --> 00:40:44,746
That's good, Lindy.
I'm happy for you.

895
00:40:44,833 --> 00:40:47,662
[chuckles]
I feel stupid.

896
00:40:47,749 --> 00:40:49,403
I don't know why
I'm telling you.

897
00:40:49,490 --> 00:40:53,102
It's just I don't really have
anyone else to say it to

898
00:40:53,189 --> 00:40:54,626
now that he's gone.

899
00:40:56,279 --> 00:40:58,847
Well, I know
a social worker.

900
00:40:58,934 --> 00:41:00,370
I'll get you her number.

901
00:41:00,458 --> 00:41:02,460
There's all sorts of programs
and shelters.

902
00:41:02,547 --> 00:41:04,418
She can help you.

903
00:41:06,202 --> 00:41:07,987
Thank you.

904
00:41:08,074 --> 00:41:09,858
[sighs]
Sure.

905
00:41:13,471 --> 00:41:14,994
Hey, how did you know
Billy was having an affair

906
00:41:15,081 --> 00:41:16,952
with the teacher?

907
00:41:17,039 --> 00:41:18,650
They both had leaves
on them

908
00:41:18,737 --> 00:41:21,174
from their make-out spot
in the woods.

909
00:41:21,261 --> 00:41:22,610
That's it?

910
00:41:22,697 --> 00:41:24,090
Well, plus I could see
she liked him.

911
00:41:24,177 --> 00:41:26,440
She was meaner to him
than the other kids.

912
00:41:26,527 --> 00:41:28,355
You know, like Lisbon
is to me.

913
00:41:28,442 --> 00:41:29,748
Shut up!

914
00:41:29,835 --> 00:41:31,358
See?

915
00:41:31,445 --> 00:41:32,925
[phone beeps]

916
00:41:33,012 --> 00:41:34,796
Oh, a text from Rigsby.

917
00:41:34,883 --> 00:41:36,537
[phone vibrating]

918
00:41:36,624 --> 00:41:37,756
[phones beeping]

919
00:41:39,279 --> 00:41:40,759
It's a boy. Benjamin.

920
00:41:41,934 --> 00:41:43,065
He's so cute.

921
00:41:44,545 --> 00:41:46,591
Yeah, if Winston Churchill
was cute.

922
00:41:46,678 --> 00:41:48,070
More like Mao Tse-tung.

923
00:41:48,157 --> 00:41:49,376
Yeah. You're right.

924
00:41:49,463 --> 00:41:50,899
Hush. He's gorgeous.

925
00:41:52,031 --> 00:41:54,555
Yeah. He is gorgeous.

926
00:41:56,122 --> 00:41:58,994
[instrumental music]

927
00:42:12,399 --> 00:42:15,010
[theme music]

